new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5d8f883
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/ws2805_ln882h.md
@@ -0,0 +1,59 @@
+# WS2805 on LN882H (experimental)
+
+This driver supports 40-bit RGB+CCT pixels using the shared OpenBeken strip/UI
+backend. It is compiled with ENABLE_DRIVER_SM16703P on LN882H only. Use P6
+(PA6, SPI0 MOSI) with the existing SM16703P_DIN role. Stop other SPI strip drivers
+first. The driver rejects other pins and unsupported APB0 clock profiles.
+
+```
+SetPinRole 6 SM16703P_DIN
+SetPinRole 12 IRRecv
+SetPinRole 21 Btn_SmartLED
+SetFlag 4 1
+startDriver WS2805
+WS2805_Init 10 BRGWC
+WS2805_SetPixel all 255 0 0 0 0
+WS2805_Start
+startDriver TinyIR_NEC
+```
+
+SetPixel arguments are logical R G B C W, independently of wire order. Init
+accepts 1..255 pixels and requires a permutation of RGBCW (five unique channels).
+Pixel writes are staged; Start transmits the frame. SetRaw uses update, byte
+offset, and an even-length hex string in wire order. Invalid writes are rejected
+before any data changes. WS2805_Status logs readiness and the DMA failure count.
+Normal LED UI controls update all pixels; SmartButtonForLEDs uses that same UI
+state. NEC address/command pairs are logged and can be bound using AddEventHandler2.
+Leave P0 (microphone) unconfigured.
+
+The default SDK has APB0=40MHz. SPI /2 gives 20MHz, with 26 SPI bits per LED bit:
+zero = 7 high/19 low (350ns/950ns); one = 13 high/13 low (650ns/650ns).
+Each cell is 1.30us. Eight symbols pack into exactly 26 bytes, so neither source
+bytes nor pixels introduce a gap. 800 trailing zero bytes provide 320us reset.
+This satisfies the [Worldsemi timing limits](https://datasheet.lcsc.com/lcsc/2306301035_Worldsemi-WS2805_C5446697.pdf)
+assuming continuous SPI serialization at the computed clock. The frame is
+50 source bytes, 1300 encoded bytes plus 800 reset bytes for ten pixels, 840us.
+The reset bytes are transport padding, not an extra channel per pixel.
+
+DMA completion waits for TXE and clear BSY as well as the DMA count. Errors or
+deadlines disable DMA requests/channel 4, reset SPI0, hold the data pin low for
+350us and reinitialize for the next transfer. The RTOS tick deadline is frame
+duration + 10ms + rounding; a ten-pixel transfer is bounded to about 12ms plus
+scheduling and recovery. Polling yields to other tasks; interrupts remain enabled
+for the 50us NEC sampler. WS2805 protects its buffers against concurrent QuickTick,
+HTTP and shutdown callbacks using a retained mutex (one allocation across cycles).
+
+The legacy SM16703P encoder, prescaler and platform padding are unchanged. Its
+shutdown now disables the transport before freeing the DMA source, and byte
+access/allocation failures are checked. LN882H uses software NSS/SSI and TX-only
+mode, and no longer spins indefinitely on DMA failure. The shared DMA controller
+is not reset or clock-gated, so other channels can remain active.
+
+Software checks compile and execute the actual C encoder, checked DMA path, raw
+command and NEC ISR under ARM emulation with hardware mocks. These do not emulate
+the LN882H peripheral or verify electrical timing. Hardware acceptance remains:
+scope P6, verify both whites/order, first pixel/all ten, repeated start/init/stop,
+100+ updates with the HTTP UI, simultaneous IR reception, and DMA failure recovery.
+BRGWC and the pin mapping come from the supplied factory handoff; CW order is
+still a hardware hypothesis. Firmware is a test build, not hardware validated.
+
